Scaling an AI backend from a single development process to multiple horizontal replicas (e.g., in Kubernetes or ECS) fundamentally breaks process-local assumptions. In a multi-replica deployment, identical code runs in isolated memory spaces across different machines. The following stateful subsystems must be externalized and re-engineered:

1. Shared State & Sessions: In-memory dictionaries, chat histories, and active WebSocket connections do not span processes. Conversational state and user sessions must be externalized to distributed caches (Redis) or relational databases (PostgreSQL);
2. Distributed Concurrency & Idempotency: Thread locks (threading.Lock, asyncio.Lock) only protect a single process. Replicas must use distributed locking (e.g., Redis Redlock or Postgres pg_advisory_lock) and persistent idempotency keys to prevent duplicate card charges or concurrent writes;
3. Global Rate Limiting: Process-local rate limiters (e.g., 50 req/min) multiply by the number of replicas: 10 replicas allow 500 req/min, rapidly exhausting downstream LLM provider quotas. Rate limiting must use a central Redis sliding window token bucket;
4. Connection Pool Multiplication: Every replica maintains its own database connection pool. If 10 replicas each open a pool of 20 connections, total database connections spike from 20 to 200, risking PostgreSQL max_connections exhaustion. Replicas must be sized conservatively and paired with PgBouncer connection pooling.

Running database migrations (e.g., alembic upgrade head) automatically inside application startup hooks (such as FastAPI on_event("startup") or container entrypoint scripts) is a dangerous production anti-pattern in multi-replica deployments:

1. Concurrent DDL Race Conditions: During a rolling update, multiple replicas spawn simultaneously. Each pod attempts to acquire exclusive schema locks (AccessExclusiveLock) on the database. This causes migration deadlocks, corrupted alembic_version tables, or crashed containers in a CrashLoopBackOff;

2. Breaking Backward Compatibility: If Replica 1 runs a migration that drops or renames a column while Replicas 2, 3, and 4 are still running the old code version, those older replicas immediately crash with SQL column-not-found errors;

3. Production Migration Architecture: (a) Decoupled Migration Jobs: Execute migrations in a standalone, single-instance pipeline step (e.g., a Kubernetes Job or CI/CD deployment hook) that completes successfully BEFORE new application pods are rolled out; (b) Expand-and-Contract Pattern: All schema changes must be backward-compatible with the currently running application version. To rename a column: Phase 1 adds the new column as nullable; Phase 2 deploys code that writes to both columns; Phase 3 backfills; Phase 4 deploys code reading only the new column; Phase 5 drops the old column.

Executing bulk data ingestion, PDF parsing, or embedding generation inside web application startup is an architectural failure mode:

1. Liveness & Readiness Probe Timeouts: Document ingestion and embedding generation take minutes to hours and saturate CPU/GPU resources. In Kubernetes, if a container fails to respond to liveness/readiness probes within its configured initialDelaySeconds (e.g., 30s), the kubelet marks the container unhealthy and forcibly terminates it. The pod enters a perpetual CrashLoopBackOff cycle and never starts;

2. Redundant Ingestion Multiplication: If ingestion runs on application boot, deploying 10 replicas causes all 10 replicas to ingest the identical files simultaneously, multiplying vector generation API costs by 10x and causing database row lock contention;

3. Production Architectural Separation: Web application pods must remain lean, stateless HTTP servers with sub-second startup times. Ingestion must be completely decoupled into asynchronous background pipelines: files uploaded to S3/GCS trigger event notifications (SQS/PubSub), consumed by dedicated asynchronous worker processes (Celery/Temporal/Airflow) that parse, chunk, embed, and commit to the vector database asynchronously.

In containerized production environments, a single /health endpoint is insufficient. Kubernetes and modern cloud platforms require three distinct probe contracts with fundamentally different semantics:

1. Startup Probe (/health/startup): Used during container boot. In AI services that load heavy local embedding models or warm vector caches into RAM (taking 15–45s), the startup probe grants a generous initialization window. Other probes do not execute until the startup probe succeeds;

2. Liveness Probe (/health/liveness): Tests whether the internal application process is alive and responsive (not deadlocked in an unrecoverable loop or out of memory). It performs a fast in-memory check (returning HTTP 200). CRITICAL RULE: NEVER check external dependencies (like database or Redis) in a liveness probe! If PostgreSQL experiences a temporary network hiccup, an external-checking liveness probe will fail across all 50 pods simultaneously, causing Kubernetes to kill and restart every pod at once, turning a minor database blip into a catastrophic cluster-wide crash cascade;

3. Readiness Probe (/health/readiness): Tests whether the container is currently capable of accepting incoming user traffic. It verifies that database connection pools have available slots and Redis is reachable. If the database is saturated, the readiness probe fails: Kubernetes immediately removes the pod from load balancer endpoints (so it receives no traffic), but DOES NOT kill the pod, giving it time to recover.

In distributed systems, workers processing background tasks (e.g., long-running document indexing or model evaluations) will inevitably be killed due to spot instance termination, rolling deployments, or out-of-memory (OOM) kernel kills:

1. Graceful SIGTERM Handling: When Kubernetes terminates a pod, it sends a SIGTERM signal. The worker process must trap SIGTERM, stop consuming new jobs from the queue, allow in-flight tasks a terminationGracePeriodSeconds (e.g., 30–60s) to finish, update task status in the database, and exit cleanly with code 0;

2. Stale-Job Lease / Heartbeat Recovery: If a worker process suffers a sudden hard crash (SIGKILL or kernel panic), it cannot run shutdown handlers. To prevent tasks from remaining permanently stuck in a "PROCESSING" zombie state: (a) Distributed Lease: While executing a task, the worker must update a heartbeat timestamp in Redis or the database every 15 seconds (UPDATE background_tasks SET heartbeat_at = NOW() WHERE id = :task_id); (b) Stale Reaper Daemon: A background supervisor process periodically scans for active tasks whose heartbeat_at is older than 60 seconds. The reaper marks the abandoned task as FAILED or increments attempt_count and re-enqueues it for peer workers to resume; (c) Idempotency: The task logic must be idempotent so that replaying a partially executed job does not duplicate side effects.
